=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
After exhaustive testing, the dc_clos_bgp fabric is fully healthy. Every
layer of the stack was checked: ICMP (small and 1400-byte DF), TCP, BGP
session state, kernel routing tables, iptables/nftables, and interface error
counters. All tests pass with zero packet loss and correct latency.

The technician's characterisation of super_spine_router_0 as a "black hole"
is incorrect. The super_spine has IPv4 forwarding enabled (sysctl
ip_forward=1), carries no null or blackhole routes, has no firewall rules,
and its interface counters confirm it is processing only BGP keepalive traffic
— roughly 2.5 KB in each direction. This is the expected behaviour for a
super-spine in a single-pod Clos topology: intra-pod traffic flows directly
leaf → spine → leaf, so the super_spine is never in the forwarding path for
pc_0_0 ↔ pc_0_1.

All ten BGP sessions across the fabric have been up for over eight minutes and
are exchanging the correct number of prefixes. Each leaf router has a valid
route to the remote host subnet. Both spine routers have correct next-hops
pointing directly to the leaf routers, not through the super_spine.

The observation that all traffic currently flows through spine_router_0_0
alone (spine_router_0_1 carries no data) is expected BGP best-path behaviour
in the absence of explicit multipath configuration: spine_router_0_0 has the
lower BGP router-id (172.16.0.1 < 172.16.0.3) and therefore wins the tie-
break on every equal-length path. This is a design choice, not a fault.

Root cause: no anomaly exists; the fault described in the ticket cannot be
reproduced and the network is operating correctly.

Corrective fix: no action required.
=== END ===
```